For the drugs where the generic ends up more expensive, it is on average 45\% more expensive. However, since there are only 16 drugs, an increase in price in the generic is the exception rather than the rule. 

We can look at the distribution of the percent change:

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=13cm]{Screenshot (88).png}
    \caption{Density histogram of the percent change in NADAC}
    \label{fig:nadac-gap}
\end{figure}

We can see that, for a substantial proportion of drugs in this comparison, the generic NADAC per unit is approximately 90\% lower than the brand-name NADAC per unit.

In summary, the NADAC per unit of generic drugs tend to be significantly lower than those which are strictly brand name drugs. Additionally, the NADAC per unit for strictly brand name drugs has very high variance, while the NADAC per unit for generic drugs has relatively smaller variance. In a substantial proportion of the paired comparisons, generic NADAC per unit is at least 80\% lower than brand-name NADAC per unit, although some generics have higher acquisition costs. These comparisons describe acquisition-cost gaps in the 2019 data rather than changes following generic entry.
